// property_test.ax — `forall` property-based testing (R8).
//
// A `@[test] @[forall]` function randomizes its typed parameters over N cases
// (default 100; `@[forall(n: 500)]` to override). On failure, the inputs are
// SHRUNK to a minimal counterexample and the seed is reported so the failure
// reproduces (AXON_SEED=<n>). Seeded RNG → deterministic.
//
// Run:  axon test examples/property_test.ax

// Addition commutes. Stated over `wrapping_add`, NOT `+`, and that is the
// whole point of the example: `@[forall]` generates across the FULL i64
// domain, and `+` is checked, so `a + b` panics on overflow long before
// commutativity is in question. The old version asserted `a + b == b + a`
// while its own comment said "holds for all in-range i64 pairs" — the caveat
// was written down and the generator ignored it, so the test failed 7 of 12
// unseeded runs (reproduce the original with AXON_SEED=43).
//
// A property over a PARTIAL operation must either constrain its inputs or be
// restated over a total one. `wrapping_add` is total, so this is the honest
// statement of the same law.
@[test]
@[forall(n: 300)]
fn add_commutes(a: i64, b: i64) {
    assert(wrapping_add(a, b) == wrapping_add(b, a))
}

// Doubling then halving is identity for even-derived values.
@[test]
@[forall]
fn abs_is_nonneg(a: i64) {
    assert(a == 0 - 9223372036854775807 - 1 || abs_i64(a) >= 0)
}

// String length is additive under concatenation.
@[test]
@[forall]
fn concat_len_additive(s: str, t: str) {
    let joined = axon_concat(s, t)
    assert(str_len(joined) == str_len(s) + str_len(t))
}

// A demonstrated FAILURE that shrinks: `@[test(should_fail)]` so the suite
// stays green while showing the shrinker reaches the boundary (a = 50).
@[test(should_fail)]
@[forall]
fn demo_shrink_to_boundary(a: i64) {
    assert(a < 50)
}
